  "caveats": [
    "CONVENTION: this script reads habiro-coefficients-atomic.witness.json, populated by markov_trace_symbolic in habiro_coefficients_atomic.py via schur_weyl_weight = s_λ(q,q⁻¹) / H_q(λ). That is NOT the canonical Markov trace tr_M = Σ_λ y_λ · χ^λ specified in y-lambda-closed-form-wenzl-bridge.md; the canonical y_λ is the Wenzl-bridge central coefficient. At q_0 = 1.10998 the deuteron split is canonical 0.388 (markov_peel ≡ Hoefsmit) vs Schur-Weyl-weighted 0.177 (this script's input). The canonical Term 6 path is term6_residues_at_roots.py (PR #526), which reads markov_peel.tr_M_atomic directly. Treat term6_in_MeV here as the Schur-Weyl-weighted contribution, not the canonical Term 6.",
    "Primitive roots of unity are dense in S¹, so this is NOT a complex-analytic Mittag-Leffler in the ordinary sense. tr_M is a Habiro-ring element; convergence is q-adic. Truncation at n_max=6 captures the dominant Cauchy-kernel weighted residues (smaller n → closer to q_0 = 1.11 → larger 1/|q_0 − ζ| kernel).",
    "Extend habiro-coefficients-atomic.witness.json to T, ³He, ⁴He, ⁶Li, ⁷Li, ⁹Be, ¹¹B, ¹²C, ¹⁶O before this script can answer 'does Term 6 close the A≥5 5-20% errors?' — A≥4 is blocked on symbolic Hoefsmit at H_9+, the numerical-interpolation route in habiro_coefficients_interpolate.py documents A=4 as intractable at 60 dps.",
    "Higher-order pole resolution: when l'Hôpital returns sp.oo, we run a Cauchy contour ∮_{|u-u₀|=0.05} 2u·U(u) du in u-space at u₀ = sqrt(ζ) (principal branch). Radius 0.05 is conservative for ℓ ≤ 12 (nearest neighbour at ≥ 2sin(π/ℓ²) ≥ 0.05). Pole order is detected automatically by the contour identity — no per-pole-order branching."
